    "summary": "Napari plugin for tomographic reconstruction (ASTRA Toolbox) with Paganin phase retrieval, on GPU or CPU",
    "description": "# napari-reco-plugin\n\nA [napari](https://napari.org) plugin for parallel-beam tomographic reconstruction with the\n[ASTRA Toolbox](https://www.astra-toolbox.com/), with single-distance **Paganin** phase retrieval.\n\n- Reconstruction algorithms: **FBP, BP, SIRT, SART, CGLS**, on GPU (CUDA) or CPU.\n- Standard (180° or 360°) and **half acquisition** (offset rotation axis, 360°) scans.\n- Flat/dark-field and double flat-field correction, measured angles read from `.nxs` files.\n- Centre-of-rotation tools: COR scan on one slice, automatic global COR (half acquisition).\n- **Big Data** mode: batch processing with lazy loading, result written to a temporary HDF5 file.\n- Readers for `.nxs` / `.tdf` (HDF5); writers for `.tif` and `.raw` (+ processing history as JSON).\n\nEverything also runs **without a GPU**: CuPy is optional (NumPy/SciPy are used instead) and\nASTRA's CPU algorithms are used when CUDA is not available.\n\n## Installation\n\n### pip\n\n```bash\npip install \"napari-reco-plugin[astra]\"        # plugin + ASTRA Toolbox\npip install cupy-cuda12x                       # optional: GPU arrays (or cupy-cuda11x)\n```\n\n### conda (recommended, GPU or CPU)\n\nFrom a clone of this repository, create a dedicated environment with everything\n(napari, ASTRA, CuPy, all from conda-forge) and the plugin in editable mode:\n\n```bash\nconda env create -f environment.yml\nconda activate napari-reco\nnapari\n```\n\nASTRA and CuPy use CUDA when an NVIDIA driver is present, otherwise the CPU.\nAvoid mixing Qt bindings in one environment (e.g. PyQt5 and PySide6): napari ≥ 0.10 needs Qt 6.\n\n### From source (development)\n\n```bash\ngit clone https://github.com/Clementcmoi/Reconstruction-napari-plugin.git\ncd Reconstruction-napari-plugin\npip install -e \".[testing]\"\n```\n\nThe array backend in use (`cupy (GPU)` or `numpy (CPU)`) is shown at the bottom of the\n*Reconstruction Algorithm* section. Set `RECO_PLUGIN_BACKEND=numpy` to force the CPU.\n\n## Usage\n\nStart napari, open a `.nxs`/`.tdf` file (projections, flats and darks become layers), then\n**Plugins › Reconstruction Plugin › Reconstruction**.\n\nTypical workflow in the *Reconstruction* panel:\n\n1. **Sample Selection** – choose the projection layer (angles × rows × columns). *Slice* is a\n   detector row (0 … rows − 1).\n2. **Preprocessing** – tick *Flatfield* / *Darkfield* and choose their layers. *Apply* creates a\n   `preprocess_…` layer, but it is optional: every action applies the correction itself.\n3. **Paganin** – energy (keV), distance (m), δ/β, optional unsharp mask and outlier cleaning.\n   *Apply* creates a `paganin_…_db<δ/β>` layer (optional as well).\n4. **Center of Rotation** – *Standard*: enter a COR range and *Try Center of Rotation* (one\n   frame per COR value, listed in the layer metadata). *Half acquisition*: *Find Global Center of\n   Rotation*, then refine with *Try Center of Rotation* (± delta around the value).\n5. **Reconstruction Algorithm** – algorithm, GPU or CPU, pixel size (m, also used for the units).\n6. **Process** – *Process one slice* or *Process all slices* (*Big Data* for volumes that do not\n   fit in RAM).\n\nResults carry their full processing history in the layer metadata, saved next to the image by\nthe `.tif` and `.raw` writers.\n\n### Good to know\n\n- **Parameters are read when you click.** Changing δ/β (or any field) is always taken into\n  account. A layer already filtered with Paganin is reused only if its parameters match the\n  current ones; otherwise the plugin goes back to the layer it was computed from, so Paganin is\n  never applied twice.\n- **Units:** pixel size and distance are in **metres** (0.65 µm → `0.65e-6`, 50 mm → `0.05`);\n  values that look like µm or mm are rejected, because they make δ/β seem to have no effect.\n  Commas are accepted as decimal separators. With a pixel size, reconstructions are in mm⁻¹.\n- **Without Paganin**, flat-field corrected transmissions I/I₀ are converted to attenuation\n  −ln(I/I₀) before reconstruction (Paganin's output already is an attenuation).\n- The pixel size appears in the *Paganin* and *Reconstruction Algorithm* sections: it is the\n  same field (editing one updates the other).\n- **COR convention:** standard acquisition = position of the axis on the detector (pixels);\n  half acquisition = distance from the left detector edge to the axis (the overlap is 2 × COR).\n- **SART iterations** are full sweeps over all projections.\n- **Big Data:** the temporary HDF5 output is deleted when its layer is removed or napari closes.\n  Datasets larger than `RECO_PLUGIN_LAZY_GB` (default 4 GB) are opened lazily by the reader.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n\n| Symptom | Cause / fix |\n| --- | --- |\n| Changing δ/β changes nothing | Check the units: the pixel size must be in metres (e.g. `0.65e-6`). |\n| *Use GPU (CUDA)* is greyed out | ASTRA cannot use CUDA on this machine: CPU algorithms are used. |\n| \"Another processing is still running\" | One job at a time; wait for the *Processing* window to close. |\n| \"no 1D dataset of N angles\" | Untick *Use the measured angles*, or check the file contains them. |\n\n## Development\n\n```bash\npip install -e \".[testing]\"\npytest            # CPU backend, offscreen Qt: no GPU or display needed\n```\n\nProject layout:\n\n```\nsrc/reco_plugin/\n├── _widget.py, ui_sections.py           # Qt panel\n├── _link.py                             # parameters → processing (background threads)\n├── _reader.py, _writer.py, napari.yaml  # napari reader / writers / manifest\n├── processing/                          # numerical code (Paganin, sinograms, COR, ASTRA)\n└── utils/                               # backend (CuPy/NumPy), parameters, layers, Qt helpers\ntests/                                   # pytest suite\nlaunchers/                               # Windows .bat templates to start napari\n```\n\nSee [CHANGELOG.md](CHANGELOG.md) for the list of changes.\n\n## License\n\nMIT — see [LICENSE](LICENSE). The ASTRA Toolbox itself is distributed under the GPLv3.\n",
